"NaRuto: Automatically Acquiring Planning Models from Narrative Texts | Proceedings of the AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence (https://ojs.aaai.org/index.php/AAAI/article/view/29999)\nciteturn81view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://ojs.aaai.org/index.php/AAAI/article/view/29999\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 71\nL0: cite0†Skip to main content cite1†Skip to main navigation menu cite2†Skip to site footer L1: \nL2: Open Menu\nL3: \nL4: cite3†Proceedings of the AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence L5: \nL6:   * cite4†Current L7:   * cite5†Archives L8:   * cite6†About L9:     * cite6†About the Journal L10:     * cite7†Submissions L11:     * cite8†Privacy Statement L12:     * cite9†Contact L13: \nL14: cite10†Search L15: \nL16:   * cite11†Login L17: \nL18: # NaRuto: Automatically Acquiring Planning Models from Narrative Texts\nL19: ## Authors\nL20: \nL21:   * Ruiqi Li Australian National University\nL22:   * Leyang Cui Tencent AI lab\nL23:   * Songtuan Lin Australian National University\nL24:   * Patrik Haslum Australian National University\nL25: \nL26: ## DOI:\nL27: \nL28: cite12†https://doi.org/10.1609/aaai.v38i18.29999†doi.org L29: \nL30: ## Keywords:\nL31: \nL32: PRS: Learning for Planning and Scheduling\nL33: ## Abstract\nL34: Domain model acquisition has been identified as a bottleneck in the application of planning technology, especially within narrative planning. Learning action models from narrative texts in an automated way is essential to overcome this barrier, but challenging because of the inherent complexities of such texts. We present an evaluation of planning domain models derived from narrative texts using our fully automated, unsupervised system, NaRuto.\nL35: Our system combines structured event extraction, predictions of commonsense event relations, and textual contradictions and similarities. Evaluation results show that NaRuto generates domain models of significantly better quality than existing fully automated methods, and even sometimes on par with those created by semi-automated methods, with human assistance.\nL36: ## Downloads\nL37: \nL38:   * cite13†PDF L39:   * cite14†Video/Poster L40: \nL41: ## Published\nL42: \nL43: 2024-03-24\nL44: ## How to Cite\nL45: \nL46: Li, R., Cui, L., Lin, S., & Haslum, P. (2024). NaRuto: Automatically Acquiring Planning Models from Narrative Texts. Proceedings of the AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence, 38(18), 20194–20202. https://doi.org/10.1609/aaai.v38i18.29999\nL47: \nL48: [Button: More Citation Formats ]\nL49: \nL50:   * cite15†ACM L51:   * cite16†ACS L52:   * cite17†APA L53:   * cite18†ABNT L54:   * cite19†Chicago L55:   * cite20†Harvard L56:   * cite21†IEEE L57:   * cite22†MLA L58:   * cite23†Turabian L59:   * cite24†Vancouver L60: \nL61: Download Citation\nL62:   * cite25†Endnote/Zotero/Mendeley (RIS) L63:   * cite26†BibTeX L64: ## Issue\nL65: \nL66: cite27†Vol. 38 No. 18: AAAI-24 Technical Tracks 18 L67: \nL68: ## Section\nL69: \nL70: AAAI Technical Track on Planning, Routing, and Scheduling\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFormally Verified Approximate Policy Iteration | Proceedings of the AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence (https://ojs.aaai.org/index.php/AAAI/article/view/34868)\nciteturn81view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: last week; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://ojs.aaai.org/index.php/AAAI/article/view/34868\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 65\nL0: cite0†Skip to main content cite1†Skip to main navigation menu cite2†Skip to site footer L1: \nL2: Open Menu\nL3: \nL4: cite3†Proceedings of the AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence L5: \nL6:   * cite4†Current L7:   * cite5†Archives L8:   * cite6†About L9:     * cite6†About the Journal L10:     * cite7†Submissions L11:     * cite8†Privacy Statement L12:     * cite9†Contact L13: \nL14: cite10†Search L15: \nL16:   * cite11†Login L17: \nL18: # Formally Verified Approximate Policy Iteration\nL19: \nL20: ## Authors\nL21: \nL22:   * Maximilian Schäffeler Technische Universität München\nL23:   * Mohammad Abdulaziz King's College London\nL24: ## DOI:\nL25: \nL26: cite12†https://doi.org/10.1609/aaai.v39i25.34868†doi.org L27: ## Abstract\nL28: We present a methodology based on interactive theorem proving that facilitates the development of verified implementations of algorithms for solving factored Markov Decision Processes. As a case study, we formally verify an algorithm for approximate policy iteration in the proof assistant Isabelle/HOL. We show how the verified algorithm can be refined to an executable, verified implementation. Our evaluation on benchmark problems shows that it is practical.\nL29: As part of the development, we build verified software to certify linear programming solutions. We discuss the verification process and the modifications we made to the algorithm during formalization.\nL30: ## Downloads\nL31: \nL32:   * cite13†PDF L33: \nL34: ## Published\nL35: \nL36: 2025-04-11\nL37: ## How to Cite\nL38: \nL39: Schäffeler, M., & Abdulaziz, M. (2025). Formally Verified Approximate Policy Iteration. Proceedings of the AAAI Conference on Artificial Intelligence, 39(25), 26659–26667. https://doi.org/10.1609/aaai.v39i25.34868\nL40: \nL41: [Button: More Citation Formats ]\nL42: \nL43:   * cite14†ACM L44:   * cite15†ACS L45:   * cite16†APA L46:   * cite17†ABNT L47:   * cite18†Chicago L48:   * cite19†Harvard L49:   * cite20†IEEE L50:   * cite21†MLA L51:   * cite22†Turabian L52:   * cite23†Vancouver L53: \nL54: Download Citation\nL55: \nL56:   * cite24†Endnote/Zotero/Mendeley (RIS) L57:   * cite25†BibTeX L58: ## Issue\nL59: \nL60: cite26†Vol. 39 No. 25: AAAI-25 Technical Tracks 25 L61: \nL62: ## Section\nL63: \nL64: AAAI Technical Track on Planning, Routing, and Scheduling\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nQ-Cogni: An Integrated Causal Reinforcement Learning Framework - the UWA Profiles and Research Repository (https://research-repository.uwa.edu.au/en/publications/q-cogni-an-integrated-causal-reinforcement-learning-framework/)\nciteturn81view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://research-repository.uwa.edu.au/en/publications/q-cogni-an-integrated-causal-reinforcement-learning-framework/\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 179\nL0: cite0†Skip to main navigation cite1†Skip to search cite2†Skip to main content L1: \nL2: cite3†the UWA Profiles and Research Repository Home L3: \nL4:   * cite4†Login   Link opens in a new tab†admin.research-repository.uwa.edu.au L5: \nL6: Search content at the UWA Profiles and Research Repository [Input: Search...]\nL7: # Q-Cogni: An Integrated Causal Reinforcement Learning Framework\nL8: \nL9:   * Cristiano Da Costa Cunha\nL10:   * , cite5†Wei Liu L11:   * , cite6†Tim French L12:   * , cite7†Ajmal Mian L13: \nL14:   * cite8†School of Physics, Mathematics and Computing L15:   * cite9†UWA Data Institute L16:   * cite10†Computer Science and Software Engineering L17:   * cite11†UWA Oceans Institute L18: \nL19: Research output: Contribution to journal › Article › peer-review\nL20: \nL21: cite12†1   Link opens in a new tab†www.scopus.com Citation (Scopus)\nL22: \nL23: 244 Downloads (Pure)\nL24: ## Abstract\nL25: We present Q-Cogni, an algorithmically integrated causal reinforcement learning framework that redesigns Q-Learning to improve the learning process with causal inference. Q-Cogni achieves improved policy quality and learning efficiency with a pre-learned structural causal model of the environment, queried to guide the policy learning process with an understanding of cause-and-effect relationships in a state-action space.\nL26: By doing so, we not only leverage the sample efficient techniques of reinforcement learning but also enable reasoning about a broader set of policies and bring higher degrees of interpretability to decisions made by the reinforcement learning agent. We apply Q-Cogni on Vehicle Routing Problem (VRP) environments including a real-world dataset of taxis in New York City using the Taxi & Limousine Commission trip record data.\nL27: We show Q-Cogni's capability to achieve an optimally guaranteed policy (total trip distance) in 76% of the cases when comparing to shortest-path-search methods and outperforming (shorter distances) state-of-the-art reinforcement learning algorithms in 66% of cases.\nL28: Additionally, since Q-Cogni doesn't require a complete global map, we show that it can start efficiently routing with partial information and improve as more data is collected, such as traffic disruptions and changes in destination, making it ideal for deployment in real-world dynamic settings.\nL29: Original language  | English\nL30: Pages (from-to)  | 6186-6195\nL31: Number of pages  | 10\nL32: Journal  | cite13†IEEE Transactions on Artificial Intelligence L33: Volume  | 5\nL34: Issue number  | 12\nL35: Early online date  | 3 Sept 2024\nL36: DOIs  |\nL37: \nL38:   * cite14†https://doi.org/10.1109/TAI.2024.3453230†doi.org L39: Publication status  | Published - Dec 2024\nL40: ## Access to Document\nL41: \nL42:   * cite14†10.1109/TAI.2024.3453230†doi.org L43: \nL44:   * cite15†da Costa Cunha, C. et. al. (2024) Q-Cogni: An Integrated Causal Reinforcement Learning Framework Accepted author manuscript, 3.11 MB\nL45: \nL46: ## Other files and links\nL47: \nL48:   * cite16†Link to publication in Scopus†www.scopus.com L49: \nL50: ## Fingerprint\nL51: \nL52: Dive into the research topics of 'Q-Cogni: An Integrated Causal Reinforcement Learning Framework'. Together they form a unique fingerprint.\nL53: \nL54: cite17†View full fingerprint L55: ## Cite this\nL56