ince Q-Cogni doesn't require a complete global map, we show that it can start efficiently routing with partial information and improve as more data is collected, such as traffic disruptions and changes in destination, making it ideal for deployment in real-world dynamic settings.\",\nL75: keywords = \"Artificial intelligence, Reinforcement learning, Vehicle routing\",\nL76: \nL77: author = \"\\{Da Costa Cunha\\}, Cristiano and Wei Liu and Tim French and Ajmal Mian\",\nL78: \nL79: note = \"Publisher Copyright: {\\textcopyright} 2020 IEEE.\",\nL80: \nL81: year = \"2024\",\nL82: \nL83: month = dec,\nL84: \nL85: doi = \"10.1109/TAI.2024.3453230\",\nL86: \nL87: language = \"English\",\nL88: \nL89: volume = \"5\",\nL90: \nL91: pages = \"6186--6195\",\nL92: \nL93: journal = \"IEEE Transactions on Artificial Intelligence\",\nL94: \nL95: issn = \"2691-4581\",\nL96: \nL97: publisher = \"IEEE, Institute of Electrical and Electronics Engineers\",\nL98: number = \"12\",\nL99: \nL100: }\nL101: \nL102: Da Costa Cunha, Ccite5†, Liu, W cite6†, French, T cite7†& Mian, A 2024, 'cite18†Q-Cogni: An Integrated Causal Reinforcement Learning Framework ', IEEE Transactions on Artificial Intelligence, vol. 5, no. 12, pp. 6186-6195. cite14†https://doi.org/10.1109/TAI.2024.3453230†doi.org L103: \nL104: cite18†Q-Cogni: An Integrated Causal Reinforcement Learning Framework. / Da Costa Cunha, Cristianocite5†; Liu, Wei cite6†; French, Tim et al.\nL105: In: IEEE Transactions on Artificial Intelligence, Vol. 5, No. 12, 12.2024, p. 6186-6195.\nL106: Research output: Contribution to journal › Article › peer-review\nL107: \nL108: TY - JOUR\nL109: \nL110: T1 - Q-Cogni\nL111: \nL112: T2 - An Integrated Causal Reinforcement Learning Framework\nL113: \nL114: AU - Da Costa Cunha, Cristiano\nL115: \nL116: AU - Liu, Wei\nL117: \nL118: AU - French, Tim\nL119: \nL120: AU - Mian, Ajmal\nL121: \nL122: N1 - Publisher Copyright: © 2020 IEEE.\nL123: \nL124: PY - 2024/12\nL125: \nL126: Y1 - 2024/12\nL127: N2 - We present Q-Cogni, an algorithmically integrated causal reinforcement learning framework that redesigns Q-Learning to improve the learning process with causal inference. Q-Cogni achieves improved policy quality and learning efficiency with a pre-learned structural causal model of the environment, queried to guide the policy learning process with an understanding of cause-and-effect relationships in a state-action space.\nL128: By doing so, we not only leverage the sample efficient techniques of reinforcement learning but also enable reasoning about a broader set of policies and bring higher degrees of interpretability to decisions made by the reinforcement learning agent. We apply Q-Cogni on Vehicle Routing Problem (VRP) environments including a real-world dataset of taxis in New York City using the Taxi & Limousine Commission trip record data.\nL129: We show Q-Cogni's capability to achieve an optimally guaranteed policy (total trip distance) in 76% of the cases when comparing to shortest-path-search methods and outperforming (shorter distances) state-of-the-art reinforcement learning algorithms in 66% of cases.\nL130: Additionally, since Q-Cogni doesn't require a complete global map, we show that it can start efficiently routing with partial information and improve as more data is collected, such as traffic disruptions and changes in destination, making it ideal for deployment in real-world dynamic settings.\nL131: AB - We present Q-Cogni, an algorithmically integrated causal reinforcement learning framework that redesigns Q-Learning to improve the learning process with causal inference. Q-Cogni achieves improved policy quality and learning efficiency with a pre-learned structural causal model of the environment, queried to guide the policy learning process with an understanding of cause-and-effect relationships in a state-action space.\nL132: By doing so, we not only leverage the sample efficient techniques of reinforcement learning but also enable reasoning about a broader set of policies and bring higher degrees of interpretability to decisions made by the reinforcement learning agent. We apply Q-Cogni on Vehicle Routing Problem (VRP) environments including a real-world dataset of taxis in New York City using the Taxi & Limousine Commission trip record data.\nL133: We show Q-Cogni's capability to achieve an optimally guaranteed policy (total trip distance) in 76% of the cases when comparing to shortest-path-search methods and outperforming (shorter distances) state-of-the-art reinforcement learning algorithms in 66% of cases.\nL134: Additionally, since Q-Cogni doesn't require a complete global map, we show that it can start efficiently routing with partial information and improve as more data is collected, such as traffic disruptions and changes in destination, making it ideal for deployment in real-world dynamic settings.\nL135: KW - Artificial intelligence\nL136: \nL137: KW - Reinforcement learning\nL138: \nL139: KW - Vehicle routing\nL140: \nL141: UR - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85203449122\nL142: \nL143: U2 - 10.1109/TAI.2024.3453230\nL144: \nL145: DO - 10.1109/TAI.2024.3453230\nL146: \nL147: M3 - Article\nL148: \nL149: AN - SCOPUS:85203449122\nL150: \nL151: SN - 2691-4581\nL152: \nL153: VL - 5\nL154: \nL155: SP - 6186\nL156: \nL157: EP - 6195\nL158: \nL159: JO - IEEE Transactions on Artificial Intelligence\nL160: \nL161: JF - IEEE Transactions on Artificial Intelligence\nL162: \nL163: IS - 12\nL164: \nL165: ER -\nL166: Da Costa Cunha Ccite5†, Liu W cite6†, French T cite7†, Mian A . cite18†Q-Cogni: An Integrated Causal Reinforcement Learning Framework . IEEE Transactions on Artificial Intelligence. 2024 Dec;5(12):6186-6195. Epub 2024 Sept 3. doi: 10.1109/TAI.2024.3453230\nL167: \nL168: Powered by cite19†Pure Link opens in a new tab†www.elsevier.com , cite20†Scopus Link opens in a new tab†www.scopus.com & cite21†Elsevier Fingerprint Engine™ Link opens in a new tab†ww